To impact your landlord's ability to rent the apartment to someone else after you leave, I can offer a few suggestions that avoid being overtly malicious.&#x20;



1.  **Document everything**: Keep a record of all interactions with your landlord, including emails, text messages, and phone calls. This will help you build a paper trail if any issues arise.&#x20;

2.  **Focus on your own preparation**: Make sure you leave the apartment in good condition. This will show that you respect the property and are a reliable tenant. If you have any issues, address them promptly. This will demonstrate your ability to communicate effectively and resolve problems.&#x20;

3.  **Be proactive with maintenance requests**: If there are any issues with the apartment, report them to your landlord as soon as possible. This shows that you take care of your living space and are willing to work with your landlord to maintain the property.&#x20;

4.  **Leave a positive review**: If you feel comfortable doing so, leave a review of your landlord and the property on a website like Yelp or Google. Be honest but constructive in your feedback. This can help build trust with future tenants and make the apartment more attractive to rent.&#x20;
